soc: sunxi: h713-arisc: wait for the handler's effect, not for the classifier

0091d recorded that a masked upload leaves the firmware's EDID version byte
at 0x0f where 0x01 was asked for, and left open whether the handler ORs the
bits. It does neither: the byte is this driver's doing.

SetEDIDVersion reads its argument out of the handler scratch twice - once at
0x125c4 for the log line "Host Set EDID Version.  0x%x", and again at 0x125e0
after that call has returned. Only the second read reaches the store at
0x12600, which is a plain l.andi 0x0f plus l.sb; the five accesses to
g_Data + 4 in the whole image are listed in Q13 section 6 and none of them is
a read-modify-write. arisc_wait_handler() is satisfied as soon as the
classifier has copied the payload into the scratch at 0x11680, which is
before the dispatcher and long before that second read, so arisc_send()
returns and the driver sends the next command - whose arisc_arm_handler_probe()
writes the complement of its own expected scratch over all 65 bytes. Behind
the guarded mask in arisc_hdmi_set_edid() that next command is
CheckEDIDUpdateStatus with both arguments 0, whose complement is 0xff, and
0xff & 0x0f = 0x0f. The firmware's log call is level gated and puts 28
characters on a 115200 baud sink, about 2.4 ms, against the driver's
usleep_range(500, 1000): the firmware loses by more than a millisecond, which
is why this reproduces instead of flickering (Q13 sections 1 and 2,
umbau/reviews/Q13.md).

PullHotPlug carries the identical pattern behind its own print at 0x127d4:
0x127dc re-reads the action and 0x127e4 the port.

So wait for the handler's own effect instead of for the classifier's copy,
which needs no timing argument at all. After SUB_SET_EDID_VERSION: until
g_Data + 4 (a->off.edid_version, resolved by 0091a) reads the mask that was
sent. After a PullHotPlug RESET: until the tick counter of that port reads
the 84 the handler loads at 0x123d0. Both are bounded by HANDLER_TIMEOUT_MS,
the deadline 0091 already uses for the scratch wait; on a timeout one
dev_warn names the byte that is there and the byte that was asked for and
the sequence goes on, because the byte is a readback and the status file
shows it as "held want" either way. UP only increments the counter (0x12374)
and the main loop zeroes it again as soon as it reads 1 (0x10f50..0x10f78),
and DOWN writes no SRAM at all, so neither of those two has an effect to wait
for; the settle loop stays exactly what it was for both.

arisc_hdmi_edid_mask() ends with the pulse 0091b exports, under three
conditions that are the API line: the mask differs from the one the firmware
holds, the output gate is set (an upload since the last reset), and the
bring-up sequence has run once on this boot. A publish never moves the
hotplug pin (AP3l N3), so without the pulse a changed mask stays invisible to
the source until something else moves the pin; "a re-upload is required" was
never the condition. hpd_sequence_port records the port arisc_hdmi_edid_init()
ran on so the pulse does not have to guess it.

What the HY310 sees: nothing. allwinner,edid-version-mask is absent, so the
board mask is 0 (0091c), the guard in arisc_hdmi_set_edid() finds the
firmware's byte already 0 after ResetEDIDModule and SetEDIDVersion is not
sent at all - the bring-up's mailbox traffic is byte for byte what it was,
and the new wait sits inside a function that path does not call.
arisc_hdmi_edid_mask() is reached only from the debugfs edid-version command
and the RESET wait only from debugfs hpd <port> reset; the bring-up and the
receive driver use DOWN and UP, whose code is untouched.

Follow-up to 0091/0091a/0091b/0091c/0091d, which stay byte-identical.

Tested on the HY310: not yet. The device proof is Q13's sequence on the debug
kernel - reset-edid, then "edid-version 2", then edid - where the status file
must end at "edid_version_mask: 0x02 want 0x02" and edid-port0 line 128 must
start 02 03 4c. Before this patch the same sequence gives 0x0f and 02 03 54.

diff -ruN a/drivers/soc/sunxi/sun50i-h713-arisc.c b/drivers/soc/sunxi/sun50i-h713-arisc.c
--- a/drivers/soc/sunxi/sun50i-h713-arisc.c
+++ b/drivers/soc/sunxi/sun50i-h713-arisc.c
@@ -366,6 +366,7 @@
 	bool			running;	/* handshake completed */
 	bool			edid_module_ready;	/* ResetEDIDModule done */
 	bool			hpd_sequence_done;	/* edid_init pulsed the pin */
+	unsigned int		hpd_sequence_port;	/* ... on this port */
 	int			notify_result;
 	char			notify_version[64];
 
@@ -1124,6 +1125,27 @@
 	return -ETIMEDOUT;
 }
 
+/*
+ * arisc_wait_handler() proves that the classifier copied the payload
+ * (0x11680), not that the handler has read it, and the handlers that
+ * dispatch from the scratch re-read it behind a log call (SetEDIDVersion
+ * 0x125e0, PullHotPlug 0x127dc/0x127e4). The next command's probe pattern
+ * lands in that window and replaces the argument -- a mask of 1 came back
+ * as 0x0f (Q13 section 1). So wait for the handler's own effect instead.
+ */
+static bool arisc_wait_effect(struct h713_arisc *a, u32 off, u8 want)
+{
+	ktime_t start = ktime_get();
+
+	do {
+		if (readb(sram_at(a, off)) == want)
+			return true;
+		usleep_range(500, 1000);
+	} while (ktime_ms_delta(ktime_get(), start) < HANDLER_TIMEOUT_MS);
+
+	return false;
+}
+
 /*
  * Seed the scratch with the complement of what the handler is expected to
  * leave behind, so that "unchanged" can never be mistaken for "handled" --
@@ -1695,6 +1717,11 @@
  * nothing more; the store is served with it at the next publish. Read back
  * after a masked upload the byte was 0x0f where 0x01 was asked for, which
  * the status file shows as "held want" (dev20, 22.09.2026).
+ *
+ * That 0x0f is ours: the handler re-reads its argument at 0x125e0, where
+ * the follower's probe had put 0xff, and 0xff & 0x0f = 0x0f (Q13 1); the
+ * effect wait below closes that window, and the device proof decides
+ * whether the command republishes. API line: changed value, upload, pulse.
  */
 static int arisc_edid_mask_locked(struct h713_arisc *a, u8 mask)
 {
@@ -1703,12 +1730,17 @@
 	if (ret)
 		dev_err(a->dev, "SetEDIDVersion 0x%02x not processed (%d)\n",
 			mask, ret);
+	else if (!arisc_wait_effect(a, a->off.edid_version, mask))
+		dev_warn(a->dev,
+			 "SetEDIDVersion: firmware holds 0x%02x, asked for 0x%02x\n",
+			 readb(sram_at(a, a->off.edid_version)), mask);
 	return ret;
 }
 
 int arisc_hdmi_edid_mask(u8 mask)
 {
 	struct h713_arisc *a = arisc_get(true);
+	bool pulse;
 	int ret;
 
 	if (IS_ERR(a))
@@ -1717,11 +1749,24 @@
 		return -EINVAL;
 
 	mutex_lock(&a->lock);
+	pulse = a->hpd_sequence_done &&
+		readb(sram_at(a, a->off.edid_gate)) &&
+		readb(sram_at(a, a->off.edid_version)) != mask;
 	ret = arisc_edid_mask_locked(a, mask);
 	if (!ret)
 		a->edid_mask = mask;
 	arisc_report(a, "edid-version 0x%02x rc=%d\n", mask, ret);
 	mutex_unlock(&a->lock);
+
+	/*
+	 * A publish never moves the hotplug pin (AP3l N3), so a changed mask
+	 * stays invisible to the source until the pin does (Q13 D2/D3). The
+	 * gate byte is the API line's "upload since the last reset": without
+	 * it the firmware forces the pin low anyway. The pulse takes the lock
+	 * itself, hence behind the unlock.
+	 */
+	if (!ret && pulse && arisc_hdmi_hpd_pulse(a->hpd_sequence_port))
+		dev_warn(a->dev, "mask 0x%02x: no hotplug pulse\n", mask);
 	return ret;
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(arisc_hdmi_edid_mask);
@@ -2000,6 +2045,19 @@
 	if (action == ARISC_HDMI_HPD_DOWN)
 		return 0;
 
+	/*
+	 * The same re-read behind the same log call (0x127dc/0x127e4), so wait
+	 * for the handler's effect: RESET loads the counter with 84 (0x123d0).
+	 * UP only increments it (0x12374) and the main loop zeroes it as soon
+	 * as it reads 1 (0x10f50), and DOWN writes no SRAM at all -- for those
+	 * two the settle loop below stays all there is.
+	 */
+	if (action == ARISC_HDMI_HPD_RESET &&
+	    !arisc_wait_effect(a, a->off.hpd_counter + port, HPD_RESET_COUNTER))
+		dev_warn(a->dev,
+			 "PullHotPlug RESET port %u: counter reads %u, not %u\n",
+			 port, hpd_counter(a, port), HPD_RESET_COUNTER);
+
 	start = ktime_get();
 	do {
 		cur = hpd_counter(a, port);
@@ -2109,6 +2167,7 @@
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
 
+	a->hpd_sequence_port = port;
 	a->hpd_sequence_done = true;
 	dev_info(a->dev, "EDID/HPD-Sequenz auf Port %u abgeschlossen\n", port);
 	return 0;
